WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:07.660
My goal at the end
of this talk is

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.035
you walk away
learning something,

00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:10.780
whether it's just
about an R package

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.160
or you have some new strategies
in terms of approaching some

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.320
of your more technical solutions
with end users or clients

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.360
or what have you.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.860
So a quick introduction.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.310
My name is Tanya Cashorali.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:31.450
I got my first
start with R in 2005.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.060
I was working at Children's
Hospital informatics program,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:39.580
analyzing genetic data, and
I've been using it ever since.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.000
I love R. I started my own
company based out of Boston

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.580
called TCB Analytics in 2015.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.450
And I always have
to include Porkchop.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:48.980
He's my assistant.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.710
You may see him come
in here at some point.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.472
But do feel free to contact me
through any of these avenues.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:56.930
More than happy to
answer questions

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.430
if we don't have
enough time either.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.750
So what is anomaly detection?

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.620
This is just the
Wikipedia definition.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.650
It's sometimes referred
to as outlier detection,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.830
but I like to say it gets
a little fancier than just

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.100
detecting standard deviations
above or below a mean.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.440
It's a way to identify
rare items or events.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.390
I typically use it
for time-series data.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.030
Twitter actually developed the
package, which I'll get into

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.310
and they use it to look
at tweets per second.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.900
You can use it for CPU
metrics and utilization,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.700
but I'll talk about
two different industry

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.760
cases in which we
were able to use

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.650
and had a lot of
success doing so.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:43.400
So the R package, like I said,
was developed first at Twitter,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.968
and there's some
really good papers

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:46.260
that I link at the end of this.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:47.850
The slides are available.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.032
I definitely recommend it.

00:01:49.032 --> 00:01:50.490
There's some good
reading in there.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.823
I won't have time to get into
all the technical details,

00:01:52.823 --> 00:01:56.540
but I actually used a
fork from hrbrmstr

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.790
and he made a few modifications
to the package, one of which

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.850
is it returns tidy
data frames, tibbles,

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.810
and he also made the function
names a little easier snake

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:15.350
case aliases so you can see them ad ts
for time series and ad vec for vector.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:16.560
So this is the package.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.350
It's been phenomenal
for us so far.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.470
Now just to talk about
the implementation

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.830
or the underlying
algorithm, very briefly,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:32.450
it builds upon an Extreme Studentized
Deviate test, just a big word

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.870
for saying it does some
time series decomposition

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.110
and robust statistics.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.570
And the paper I mentioned
actually benchmarks

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:43.970
these four approaches, linear,
a seasonal decomposition

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.960
with loss, and a quant spline.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.900
The one they said
actually performed

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.720
the best was piecewise median.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.363
So it looked like
stable metrics typically

00:02:54.363 --> 00:02:56.780
exhibited very little change
in the median over two-week

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:57.510
windows.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.470
So it defaults to breaking
your data into two week windows

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.410
and calculating a moving median.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:06.080
It also accounts for intraday
and weekly seasonalities.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.010
It was pretty much performing
on par with these more

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:11.310
expensive calculations.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.980
It's four times faster
than the other ones.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.130
So that's the red line
you're seeing here.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.440
We've had no performance
issues and have given it

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.730
quite a bit of data and we run
it everything in real time.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.250
So I love that
this track is also

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:31.090
called working with people
because it's the hardest

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.900
thing we do. R code

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.980
and math is typically easy
compared to working with people

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.460
and that's what
we do day to day.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.320
Typically, the problems
when implementing

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.360
complex technical
solutions comes down

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.640
to communication breakdown.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.120
It always comes down to that
between developers and end

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.800
users typically.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.750
So oftentimes we'll get a
set of initial requirements.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.380
And when building data
products, they almost always

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:58.360
change or get thrown
out the window.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.857
There's just so much
that any end user,

00:04:00.857 --> 00:04:03.190
I don't care how much you
know the product or the data,

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.440
there's so many things you'll
never be able to predict

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.015
and that could happen as soon
as you start building. Or an app

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:09.890
gets into production and

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.230
it just doesn't meet the
end users' expectations.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.530
They may have given you
a mockup or a wireframe,

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.540
but there were a whole
bunch of other edge cases

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.250
that they just didn't think of
that they want to then tackle.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.350
And we see that happen a lot.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.960
And this happens because
not all users have

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.400
the technical understanding.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.192
Sometimes they have a
limited understanding

00:04:28.192 --> 00:04:31.260
of the statistical
processes and principles,

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.750
and then developers may just
take those user requirements

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.700
and run with them and
they disappear and come

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.040
back with an end product.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.860
And lastly, developers are not
always including those users.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.220
So you don't want to disappear,

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.700
and then just come back
to the user with something

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.600
that they may have
not actually wanted.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.910
So part of that art is figuring
out exactly what the user does

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:56.833
want compared to what
they're telling you.

00:04:56.833 --> 00:04:58.500
And that's where
Shiny comes into play.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.410
So we've had a lot of success
just taking ideas, connecting

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.320
to real data and showing
the users something

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.550
tangible, something that
they can interact with.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.470
And what we found is using
Shiny as a collaboration aid,

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.670
we see those requirements
quickly change.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.450
We see the user get
excited because they're now

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.120
touching something real and
working with something real,

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.457
and they feel more engaged.

00:05:21.457 --> 00:05:23.540
They feel a sense of
ownership that typically they

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.707
wouldn't have if you were
just a developer off doing

00:05:25.707 --> 00:05:28.985
your own thing and disappearing
for weeks or months

00:05:28.985 --> 00:05:30.860
and then coming back
with something that they

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.640
didn't necessarily feel
like they were a part of.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.010
So this is what I like to
call rapid prototyping,

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:40.190
and it enables you to
demonstrate value before doing

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:41.600
this full scale implementation.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.690
It enables you to find all
these different edge cases.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.840
And data scientists can focus
on problem solving instead

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.480
of having to play this
back and forth of, well,

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:51.540
what exactly do you want?

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.280
You can solve a problem quickly.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.010
Come back, show the
client something,

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:56.970
and then keep
iterating that way.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.090
So to get into the examples,
the first one I think

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.060
is pretty cool.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.600
It's a

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.010
hardware manufacturer
in life sciences.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:12.560
They essentially build the
rapid blood testing machines

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:13.380
that in hospitals.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.640
So if you go to the hospital,
hopefully not when you're there and you

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.020
get your blood taken.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.330
This is the company
creating the devices that

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.780
give you those rapid results.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.510
They have sensors in them that
relay quantitative metrics

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.620
and help diagnose
instrument failures.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.340
But what this
company was doing was

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.470
deploying the
hardware in the field

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:36.232
and finding out retroactively
that there was a problem.

00:06:36.232 --> 00:06:37.690
So they would take
the machine back

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:41.270
and analyze all of the data
coming off the machines.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.500
This seemed like a
perfect opportunity

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:44.580
to use anomaly detection.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.210
We wanted to identify those
problems before they went out

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.890
into the field.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.810
So here's what the data and
experimentation looks like.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.910
We have various metrics
coming off these machines.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:58.410
There are things like
protein levels and analytes,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:02.850
and they come off at different
aggregations 24 hours,

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.050
12 hours, et cetera.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.760
This anomaly detection function
has a bunch of parameters.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.000
And we just played
around with some of them

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.180
and iterated on things like
what percentage of the data

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:15.400
should we capture anomalies for?

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.930
Should it be positive
and negative direction?

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:20.950
So above or below the
expected threshold.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.160
The level of significance.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.910
Maybe we really need
to be stringent here.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:28.830
Maybe we can only go with
90% confidence versus 99%

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.930
And then that piecewise median
period that I mentioned.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.720
Another thing we realized is
there are some outliers that

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.270
are just insane and anomaly
detection would  always pick up,

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:40.840
and that's very indicative
of a hardware failure.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.920
But the client already
knew about these.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.140
They said, well, we know
that these are problematic.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:48.070
So we actually did
some outlier removal

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.970
and then ran the
anomaly detection.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:53.090
And of course then there
were no anomalies detected.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:57.835
The input data very much makes a
difference on your end results.

00:07:57.835 --> 00:07:59.960
So those are all parameters
we wanted to play with.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:03.820
And we were literally
manually analyzing all of this

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:07.060
and it turns out putting
it into a Shiny app

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.280
and just allowing
myself and the client

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.450
to play with these
different parameters.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:13.540
We quickly got feedback
on, hey, this actually

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.227
looks a good threshold to use.

00:08:15.227 --> 00:08:16.310
These are good parameters.

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:18.970
Let's run with these.

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:20.680
From there we
thought, well, instead

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.462
of a sort of top down
approach and exploring

00:08:23.462 --> 00:08:25.420
all the different options,
let's alert the user

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:26.860
and exactly where to go.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.820
So we took the high significance
anomalies, highlighted them

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:33.620
and allowed the user
to then click one.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.770
So I want to more
about this one.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.059
What's going on with
potassium or calcium?

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:40.539
And it would bring you right
back to that screen

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:42.130
pre-populate all the
parameters, again just

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:45.467
a nice little user experience
thing, time saver and they

00:08:45.467 --> 00:08:47.175
could dig into the
details a little more.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.590
The code was pretty
straightforward.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.480
The input data looks just like
you'd expect for a time series.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.660
There's a time
stamp and a measure.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.050
We iterated over the
different significance values.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.420
We also defaulted to
using one year of data

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.400
prior to the date
range of the client

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.670
is actually interested in.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:10.650
So if I care about May
of 2020, let's start

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.830
with data from May 1st of 2019
to get some of that seasonality

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.540
and more data is better
for this type of algorithm.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.920
But throughout the project
they then informed us, hey,

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:25.600
we actually have these
limits that we already know.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.510
It's like you consider
it a prior in Bayesian  math.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:30.170
And so.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:30.930
Upper limit,

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:33.080
lower limit is something
that they would say

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:34.590
these are automatically bad.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.560
So we thought, well,
this seems like it's

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:39.245
guiding us a little more and we
could probably integrate this.

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:41.120
First, we start with a
completely data driven

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.450
approach, agnostic from
any business logic.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:47.610
But then we said, well, maybe
we can incorporate these limits.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:50.570
And even giving them a
simple tool like this

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:52.050
to explore those
different outliers

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.550
was something that
they didn't have.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.490
And they were really doing
this all pretty manually

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:56.065
and by hand.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:02.720
So then we combine those rules
with the anomaly detection.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:04.670
And this is over a
course of a few months,

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:07.500
we got better and better
refining these parameters.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.620
So now the colors you
see here, the green

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:11.540
is just an anomaly
that the algorithm

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.480
found based on the parameters
we already tweaked.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.390
The blue means it's approaching
that threshold limit.

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:19.280
And these lines,
these vertical lines

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:23.450
are those thresholds
which vary by analyte.

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:25.530
And then the red is both.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:27.890
So we decided these
are probably most

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:30.770
important to look at because
there's strong signal here.

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.180
Not only are you approaching
limit, but it's the anomaly

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:36.530
detection is saying this
is trending differently

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:38.050
based on this piecewise median.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:43.040
The other thing we
did was allow the user

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:45.260
to specify how
stringent they wanted

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:46.650
this approaching limit to be.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.500
So here you can see it's
not that close to the line.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.470
We were able to
define thresholds.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:54.140
Like maybe they really want
it to get close and the way

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:56.250
we did that was
based on quartiles.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.310
So the closer they
were to the quartile

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:01.400
of that range, the top quartile
that was more stringent

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:02.230
and so on.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.940
And we let them kind
of open up the lens,

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.930
so to speak, to be
looser, more stringent.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:11.240
This ended up being the best
approach for the client,

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:13.730
just not only was
it the algorithm,

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:17.285
but incorporating the
business logic as well.

00:11:17.285 --> 00:11:19.160
So I want to jump quickly
into the next example

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:22.070
so that we have
time for questions.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.440
The other example, and
I should wrap up also

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:26.450
that they're using this
on a day to day basis.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:27.830
It runs as a Shiny app.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:31.160
They're able to connect
live too their database.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:35.060
And it's been used as a
tool to basically direct

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.310
their investigation and
identify these hardware

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:40.410
failures before the machines
get shipped out to the hospital.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.150
So we thought we think that's
a really cool application.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:46.250
And it's R and Shiny.

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.120
The price tag is pretty good.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:52.060
So real-world example
again is another client

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.290
that works in the sports
and entertainment business.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:57.880
They actually do market research
around sports technology.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:02.080
So things like in-venue
betting and understanding

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:06.340
how to engage fans in
this new technical age.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:08.590
Biometric sensors on
athletes, you name it,

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:11.208
basically any sports
technology and I

00:12:11.208 --> 00:12:13.000
like to call them kind
of a market research

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.830
company for sports tech.

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:18.880
So what they have is a
whole bunch of news articles

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.360
that they collect
via Feedly, diffbot,

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.950
but these are different
third-party tools.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:28.780
And Leo is something that's,
I believe, part of Feedly

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:32.500
and it analyzes all the
information for you.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:36.220
It's kind of like a smart filter
to bring in the information

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:38.440
take care of.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.880
So the data is thousands
of sports news articles.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:45.180
There's also entity tagging
that this Leo tool does

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.870
it tags sports teams
technologies vendors

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:52.590
meaning vendors like
Google, Microsoft, Nike.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.960
Technologies being
anything from blockchain

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.140
to augmented reality.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:12:59.910
And so what we
thought here was they

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.820
wanted interesting news
stories to be surfaced to them.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:06.280
So we simply took the
number of mentions

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:07.930
of these various
entities over time.

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:10.930
So you can imagine Boston Red
Sox are mentioned 10 times

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:11.540
a week.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:13.170
Suddenly they're mentioned 30.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:14.580
Let's investigate why.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.530
Here's what the first
iteration looked like.

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:22.110
We have a date range.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:22.830
We have.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:25.380
They can filter by
league if they'd like.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:26.940
We're looking at
all leagues here

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:30.300
and we have a list of anomalies
that the R package bubbled up

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.130
to the top.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:36.300
The Seattle Sounders
here were detected.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:39.610
We click that and it
gives us a time series.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:42.240
Look at that particular anomaly.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.460
We can then click
it and discover

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:47.250
more about what's happening.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.590
So on that date
in Shiny pops up,

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:55.140
this nice little module, the
Seattle Sounders and Amazon

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:56.120
worked together.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:59.850
Amazon had acquired streaming
rights for Prime Video soccer

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:03.000
matches, and that's a huge
thing that this company would

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.260
like to know about.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.930
So from there you can see
how you can rapid prototype

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:11.530
and it might be manual in
your surface some anomalies

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:13.660
but you could imagine how
this could be automated

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:16.150
and to report, give me the
five most interesting stories

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:17.110
that week.

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:20.600
Give me the teams that are
spiking in the news and so on.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:27.650
Another cool use case
for them was we used it.

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:30.160
It doubled as a QC tool,
so some certain things

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:32.140
would be spiking
that shouldn't be.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:34.330
For example, the
Ottawa Senators,

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:37.090
they weren't doing
particularly well.

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:38.590
There was really
no reason it wasn't

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:40.350
like the playoffs or anything.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:44.630
This is an NHL team, so
we decided to dig into it.

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.660
And it turned out
that diffbot was

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:49.810
tagging OTT for the
Ottawa Senators

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:51.640
because that's our abbreviation.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.650
But it also stands for
Over the Top Streaming

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:55.030
Services.

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:58.100
So we had identified a
problem with the tagging.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:14:59.800
We alerted diffbot,
and we got that all

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:02.530
fixed on the data itself.

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:05.260
So it can also be
used as a data QC tool,

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:08.800
not only to surface interesting
things, but potentially

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.860
problematic things, as we talked
about also in the first example

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:13.665
of finding hardware failures.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:20.390
And I'm leaving, I think, at
least 4 minutes for questions.

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:23.830
But final thoughts again,
working with people is the hardest

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:27.460
thing. We're lucky
that usually they're

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:30.220
people we've worked with so
far understand the problem

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.110
and they're very collaborative.

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:36.250
And I think being able to show
them these results piecemeal

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:38.980
is what builds up the confidence
and their understanding

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:41.120
of the tool and why it matters.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:44.320
Giving them the why or allowing
them to dig in to the why

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:48.370
and explore more in their own
is that sort of self service

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:52.900
and freedom of analysis that
they need to really feel

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:54.110
like they're engaged.

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:55.990
So Shiny has been just
incredible for that

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:58.060
because I remember the
days where you just

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:01.660
had to show them static charts
and put together a PowerPoint.

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:04.690
And it's very much like
I'm just talking to you

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.530
rather than working together.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.320
So anomaly detection is
a useful data driven tool

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:11.790
when used appropriately.

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:15.270
In our cases, we had
identified specific needs

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:18.600
for each scenario and
tailored them accordingly.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.640
The first use case
was for proactively

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.410
identifying hardware failures.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:24.900
We learned over time that
incorporating their threshold

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.690
limits would actually give
us more accurate results

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:31.150
rather than just relying on
the data. And the other use case

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:31.650
surface

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:35.830
interesting news stories as well
as doubled as a data QC tool.

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.960
Work always work closely
with your end users

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.590
via that iterative process.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:42.730
Shiny again is
fantastic for this.

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:45.130
I always say build
fast and fail quickly.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:47.470
I'd rather show you something
that you think is terrible

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:48.830
and you tear it apart.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:52.130
And getting that constructive
criticism to me is a good sign.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:53.430
It means you're invested.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:54.860
It means you have ideas.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:59.108
And it means that we can
improve upon it very fast.

00:16:59.108 --> 00:17:01.150
And then don't leave out
important business logic

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:02.218
where it can help you.

00:17:02.218 --> 00:17:04.510
We don't always have to just
rely on statistical models

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:06.920
and assume it's going to
solve all our problems.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:09.310
There's often times that we
can incorporate that domain

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:11.690
knowledge and it can
really improve our results.

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:14.890
So always be looking for
those pieces of logic that

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:18.449
can help your automation or
your math or your R package.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:22.430
And questions.

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:24.500
Feel free again to
contact me here.

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:27.339
I look forward to hopefully
being there next year

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:31.290
and enjoy the rest
of the conference.

